            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of codeine phosphate and morphine sulphate in infants undergoing cleft palate repair.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>528-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To ascertain the quality of analgesia provided by morphine in comparison to codeine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study is a prospective, randomized, double-blind trial of analgesic effect employing validated pain scores.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Infants having primary cleft palate repair with informed parental consent to enter the study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Infants received one of two analgesics intraoperatively for immediate postoperative pain relief. Morphine was given by intravenous injection and codeine by the intramuscular route.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pain scores in the immediate postoperative period for 2 hours following surgery; this outcome measure was decided prior to data collection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The pain score and other outcome measures were all blinded. Measurements are all evident from the nature of the results.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There was no clinically significant difference observed in the analgesic effect of either drug on the two groups studied.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
